# SATA link power management. Installed to
# /etc/udev/rules.d/60-steam-machine-power.rules.
#
# Applies to every ahci host rather than to host0/host1 specifically, so a disk
# moved to a different port keeps the setting. host2-host5 are empty; setting a
# link policy on a port with nothing attached is a no-op.
#
# Why udev and not a one-shot write at boot: the attribute is per scsi_host, and
# hosts are created when the ahci driver binds. A boot script would have to race
# that; a udev rule is applied as each host appears, and re-applied on any
# subsequent `change` event.
#
# `med_power_with_dipm` is the policy powertop asks for and the one TLP uses by
# default on SSDs. It enables Host Initiated Power Management down to Slumber
# plus Device Initiated Power Management, so the link drops to low power when
# idle and either end can bring it back. libata negotiates DIPM against the
# drive's advertised capability, so a drive that does not support it silently
# gets the HIPM half rather than a broken link.
#
# The risk, and why --status checks for it: LPM interacts badly with a minority
# of SATA SSDs, producing "failed command: READ FPDMA QUEUED" and a link reset
# under load. On this machine the two drives are a btrfs RAID1 holding the game
# library (see ../../hardware/storage/), so a reset would surface as filesystem
# errors rather than something subtle. `install.sh --status` greps the journal
# for ATA link resets and exception messages for exactly this reason.
#
# Reverting is a runtime write, no reboot:
#   for h in /sys/class/scsi_host/host*/link_power_management_policy; do
#       echo max_performance > "$h"
#   done
# and `./install.sh --uninstall` does it for you.
ACTION=="add|change", SUBSYSTEM=="scsi_host", KERNEL=="host*", \
    ATTR{link_power_management_policy}="med_power_with_dipm"
